alue in betting. The other player has been way too passive.\n\n**Evaluation:** Check (0)\n**Reason:** The opponent has shown zero strength. Checking preserves the pot and wins the small pot often.\n**Answer:** 0\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold a pair of 8s (8 of Clubs and the board\'s 8 of Hearts) with King, Queen, and 10 as kickers. The board is wet with straight possibilities (9-J makes a straight), and there are three overcards to your 8 (King, Queen, 10). However, the game history shows extreme passivity; both you and your opponent have checked every single round (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn, and now River). This suggests your opponent likely does not have a King, Queen, or 10, as they would typically bet those for value or protection. They likely have a weak hand, missed draw, or a lower pair. Your pair of 8s has significant showdown value in this specific "checked-down" scenario. Betting has little upside: better hands (K, Q, 10) will call and beat you, while worse hands (air, lower pairs) will likely fold, meaning you don\'t extract extra value.\n\nReason: Checking is the optimal play. Your hand is strong enough to win at showdown against a random weak range but too weak to bet for value. Betting risks opening yourself up to a check-raise or simply folding out the hands you currently beat. Taking the free showdown secures the pot if your hand is best without risking more chips.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
